From c0f9d8d795802d76e4003458b32b28b026d86252 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Neil Johnson <njohnson@roku.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 14:01:08 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 06/26] aslink: raise NCPS to 256 to match the assemblers,
 fixing symbol collisions

NCPS - the number of significant characters in an identifier - is
80 in linksrc/aslink.h but 256 in asxxsrc/asxxxx.h. Both getid()
implementations (lklex.c and aslex.c) silently truncate at NCPS-1,
so the assembler writes symbol and area names up to 255 characters
into the .rel file and the linker then reads only the first 79 of
each. Two distinct names sharing a long prefix become one name.

The benign outcome is a spurious error. Assembling a module with
two globals that differ only after their 92nd character, both
written out in full by the assembler, the linker rejects the
second as a redefinition of the first:

  ?ASlink-Error-Multiple definition of sym_that_is_quite_long_...

The damaging outcome is silent. Where the colliding names are a
definition in one module and a reference in another, the truncated
reference matches the truncated definition, the undefined-symbol
check in symdef() never fires, and the call is quietly bound to a
different function. Reproduced with two modules, one defining
"<92-char prefix>alpha" and the other calling "<92-char
prefix>beta" which nothing anywhere defines: the unpatched linker
exits 0 with no diagnostic and emits "CD 03 00" - a call to
...alpha's address.

Names of this length are unremarkable in generated code, where a
segment prefix, a module name and a function name are concatenated
mechanically and long shared prefixes are the norm rather than the
exception.

Raise the linker's NCPS to 256 so the two limits agree and the
linker reads back exactly what the assembler wrote, and add a
comment at each definition recording that the two must be kept in
step. NCPS is used only for automatic buffers and for the strncmp()
bound in fndsym(), so this costs a few hundred bytes of stack in
the affected frames and nothing else; no structure member, file
format field or on-disk layout depends on it.

Verified with a standalone aslink built from linksrc/*.c:
- The spurious "Multiple definition" case above now links cleanly
  (exit 0) with both symbols present at distinct addresses, 0000
  and 0001, in the map.
- The silent-misbinding case now reports the fault it should
  always have reported, naming the symbol in full:
  "?ASlink-Warning-Undefined Global sym_that_..._beta referenced
  by module nb", exit 2.
- Ran the existing asz80 self-tests, tz80.bat and t80.bat
  (asz80 -gloaxf, aslink -xu), before and after: byte-for-byte
  identical .lst and .rst output.
- An ordinary two-module link produces byte-identical Intel Hex
  before and after.
- 'aslink -y' output is unchanged.
- Rebuilt with -fsanitize=address,undefined and linked a module
  declaring a 255-character area name - the longest the raised
  NCPS admits, exercising the NGSYM buffers from the preceding
  commit - with -m, -j and -y all enabled: no sanitizer reports.

Note this makes the two tools agree; it does not make truncation
itself diagnosable. A name longer than 255 characters is still
silently clipped, identically, by both.

Folded in, asxdoc, asxhtml: a name may be 255 characters, not 79:

The manual says a module, area or bank name "may be from 1 to 79
characters in length", in three places.  That was true of the
round trip rather than of the assembler:  the assembler has always
allowed NCPS-1 characters, which is 255, but the linker's NCPS was
80, so getid() in lklex.c truncated at 79 anything it read back
from a .rel file.  A name longer than that survived assembly and
was silently cut short by the linker.

d5e2177 raised the linker's NCPS to 256 to match, which is what
removed the 79 character limit and, with it, the collisions that
truncation caused.  The manual was not updated at the time.  All
three statements now read 255.

The three sit in justified paragraphs set to 72 columns, so two
needed more than the digits changing:

- The .bank description had three spaces after "areas." - a
  sentence gap of two plus one of justification - so the extra
  character was taken from the padding and the line still ends at
  column 72.
- The .module description had no padding to give up:  its line was
  exactly 72 with single spaces throughout and one sentence gap.
  That paragraph is therefore re-wrapped, which changes how the
  justification falls across its nine lines but not what it says,
  and not how many lines it occupies.  Sentence gaps of two spaces
  are preserved.
- The .area description ends a paragraph, so its line is short and
  simply grew by one character.

Applied to the six renderings that carry the text.  asxs02.htm is
de-paginated and set to 64 columns rather than 72, so its copy of
the .module paragraph was re-wrapped to its own width.

Verified:
- No occurrence of "1 to 79 characters" remains anywhere under
  asxdoc or asxhtml.
- No line count changed in any file, so the pagination is
  untouched:  asmlnk.txt is still 700 pages with no page over 55
  lines, and asmlnk.rtf still has 32516 \par and 700 \page,
  matching the text line for line, with its unescaped brace
  balance still 0.
- asmlnk.htm still agrees with asmlnk.txt at its six line offset,
  and the asxdoc and asxhtml copies of each file remain identical.
- Every line of the re-wrapped paragraph is exactly 72 columns,
  bar the last, as before.

